  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2{)}^{2}}.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is equal to ${f}^{\u2032}(3).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2{)}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-4x+(-2(x-2{)}^{-3}) \\\\  & =-4x-\\frac{2}{(x-2{)}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(3) & =-4(3)-\\frac{2}{(3-2{)}^{3}} \\\\  & =-12-2 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=3$ is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{\u2032}(3)=-14.\n$$",
